\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee gains stalled by cbank - traders   \b0\par\par\uc2 386 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2007\par 04:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, June 18 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee rose to its strongest level in nearly a week on Monday, powered by strong foreign investment flows, though dollar purchases by oil refiners and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  limited its gains, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> ended at 40.7725/7825 per dollar, rising from Friday's 40.86/87. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a nine-year high of 40.28 hit in late May.   \par \par \uc2 "The RBI was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  today, but not aggressively because they have to be careful not to exacerbate the already loose cash conditions," said a senior dealer with a private bank, referring to the Reserve Bank of India.   \par \par \uc2 Overnight interbank loan rates <INROND=> were trading at 3.00/3.25 percent on Monday, an indication of a surfeit of cash in the banking system. Call rates trade at around 6-7 percent when cash supplies are normal.   \par \par \uc2 Dealers said there was strong overseas interest in the rupee, with sentiment buoyant in anticipation of a surge in investment flows into the fast-growing economy.   \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, when the rupee approached its peak of 40.71. The market suspects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of having nudged it back to 40.74, after which oil refiners bought dollars, further weighing on the local unit.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee has gained about 8.5 percent against the dollar this year, and would have moved even higher had it not been for suspected central \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap its gains, analysts say.   \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $2.06 billion through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the rupee in check in April, taking its total purchases to $24 billion for the six months from November. It is widely suspected of selling rupees over the past month to keep the dollar above 40.50 rupees.   \par \par \uc2 "Barring any significant weakness in the stock market, the rupee should trade higher than 41 per dollar this month," said the senior dealer.   \par \par \uc2 "But the RBI seems committed to seeing that it won't gain past 40.50 on the other end," he added.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par                    MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|RBN|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070621e36i0004u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
