\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee eases on oil demand, cbank concerns   \b0\par\par\uc2 401 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2007\par 04:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to open)   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, May 30 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee eased away from a recent nine-year high on Wednesday, as investors pared positions in the local unit on concerns over \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and on dollar demand from oil refiners for month-end payments.   \par \par \uc2 At 9:35 a.m. (0405 GMT), the rupee <INR=IN> was at 40.555/565 per dollar, slipping from Tuesday's 40.48/49, which was its strongest close since May 1998, and well clear of Monday's nine-year intra-day high of 40.28.   \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been blocking the rupee at 40.50, so the market is likely to be a rangebound today," said a dealer with a private bank, who expects the rupee to trade in a 40.50-40.60 range.   \par \par \uc2 Dealers said there were traces of Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the rupee touched 40.46 on Tuesday, but said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar buying was relatively light compared with an estimated $600-$650 million it bought on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 Lower inflation gave the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more elbow room to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they said. Data on Friday showed annual inflation eased to an eight-month low of 5.27 percent in mid-May.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee is Asia's best performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar this year, gaining more than 9 percent on strong capital inflows into the fast-growing economy.   \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the rupee was likely to appreciate in coming weeks, buoyed by a wave of capital inflows slated for Indian equities, including an IPO by property developer  \uc2 DLF Ltd.\uc2 , that is looking to raise an Indian record of $2.4 billion.   \par \par \uc2 "With around $10 billion of IPOs slated for next month in India, and foreign interest likely to be substantial, the rupee-positive flow picture does not look like changing any time soon," Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with  \uc2 ABN AMRO Bank\uc2  in Singapore, said in a note.   \par \par \uc2 "Given the continued sensitivity to inflation, we do not see a high likelihood of the RBI trying to draw a line in the sand if flows come through strongly in June," added Jalinoos, who expects the rupee to test 40.00 in June.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par \par                    MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|SNI|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070602e35u0001p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
