\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee knocked of 9-yr peak by cbank-traders   \b0\par\par\uc2 344 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2007\par 04:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, May 28 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee was driven back from a nine-year peak on Monday by aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a corporate order had pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through a level that had been defended last week, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk, but traders said the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (RBI) bought about $600-$650 million after the rupee surged to 40.28 per dollar, its highest since May 1998, on heavy dollar selling by a software services firm.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee <INR=IN> ended at at 40.51/52, close to last week's peak of 40.50 and higher than Friday's closing 40.55/56.   \par \par \uc2 "Once dollar-rupee broke 40.50, a level it's been hovering around recently, the market tried to test the RBI, and it got a conclusive answer," said the chief dealer with a private bank.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI, which had bought $22 billion in the five months to end-March trying to stem the rupee's strength, started buying dollars aggressively at 40.30, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 Lower inflation gave the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more elbow room to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they said. Data on Friday showed annual inflation eased to an eight-month low of 5.27 percent in mid-May.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee is Asia's best performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar this year, gaining over 9 percent, driven higher by robust capital inflows into the fast-growing economy.   \par \par \uc2 The market is expecting $4 to $5 billion of foreign money to flow into equities by the end of June, including for an IPO by property developer  \uc2 DLF Ltd.\uc2  that aims to raise an Indian record $2.4 billion.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have bought nearly $3.7 billion of local equities this year, after investing nearly $8 billion in the previous year.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par                    MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 4)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|E|RBN|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070531e35s0004v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
