\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Indian Rupee Off New 9-Yr Highs On RBI  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 486 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2007\par 10:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Recasts lead, adds analyst comments and details of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee Monday rose to a fresh nine-year high against the U.S. dollar, but gave up most of its gains as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely stepped up dollar purchases, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar plunged to an intra-day low of INR40.28 earlier in the session on strong capital inflows in to India.   \par \par \uc2 At 0800GMT, the dollar was trading at INR40.53 after the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem the rupee's sharp rise, dealers said. The dollar had closed at INR40.59 on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 "The foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows in to India are heavy, and this accompanied by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's absence from the market has pushed the dollar down," said a dealer at a state-run bank, who asked not to be identified.   \par \par \uc2 Traders expect the rupee to rise further on continued foreign fund inflows ahead of a couple of large initial public offers.   \par \par \uc2 Foreigners have been actively buying local stocks, pushing the Bombay Stock Exchange's benchmark 30-stock Sensex close to its all-time high of 14,723.88 on Feb. 9.   \par \par \uc2 At 0800GMT, the index was up 1% at 14,474.97.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have bought local shares worth $791 million so far this month, according to the latest data posted on the Securities and Exchange Board of India's website.   \par \par \uc2 However, after its absence from the market earlier Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  resumed selling rupees, to help blunt some of the impact of the foreign flows, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 "The Reserve Bank of India is back in the market, which has helped the dollar," said another dealer with a private bank.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI has bought $2.31 billion from the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in March, after mopping up a record $11.86 billion in February.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said they expect the dollar to remain weak against the rupee in the near term.   \par \par \uc2 "Most traders are going short on dollars on hopes that foreign fund flows will remain strong in the coming days," said Pankaj Sharma, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Union Bank of India.   \par \par \uc2 "If the trend continues, the dollar may fall to INR40.00 by the end of the week," he said.   \par \par \uc2 The steady flow of foreign money is unlikely to slow anytime soon as stock markets brace for large public issues, including the initial public offer of DLF Ltd., India's largest real estate company by the number of properties, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 Besides DLF, which could raise as much as about $2.5 billion through its issue in June, ICICI Bank (532174.BY) also plans to sell shares to the public next month.   \par \par \uc2 -By Neelabh Chaturvedi, Dow Jones Newswires; 91-22-22884212-18; neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Alpana Sarma [ 28-05-07 0804GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument AER0000020070528e35s0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
