\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW:India  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Must Curb Rupee Rise -Tarapore   \b0\par\par\uc2 666 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2007\par 10:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately to stem the rupee's sharp rise against the U.S. dollar to avoid hurting economic growth, said S.S. Tarapore, a former deputy governor of the Reserve Bank of India.   \par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee is currently trading at nine-year highs against the dollar helped by overseas borrowings by domestic companies and foreign capital inflows into stocks, local companies and infrastructure development.   \par \par \uc2 "If you allow the real effective exchange rate to appreciate, you are appreciating against fundamentals. Your interest rate is higher, your inflation rate is higher and you try to be stronger than those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  (in the basket). It will kill industry," Tarapore, who is also a member of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  advisory committee on monetary policy, told Dow Jones Newswires in a recent interview.   \par \par \uc2 According to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which looks at a six-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade-weighted index, the rupee was overvalued by 12% on April 18. A  \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  index estimates the rupee is overvalued by around 17%.   \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has traditionally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the rupee was overvalued above the 5% level, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 Tarapore said the real effective exchange rate appreciation was not a correct instrument for containing inflation.   \par \par \uc2 "It is the worst possible instrument. Generalized inflation means there is lot of liquidity in the system and the remedial measure is to cut back on monetary expansion," Tarapore said.   \par \par \uc2 He advocated the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and consequent sterilization through cash reserve ratio hikes and market stabilization scheme bonds.   \par \par \uc2 "The MSS or sterilization through cash reserve ratio has a cost. But we are not understanding or calculating the cost of non-sterilization as it is in terms of devastation of the economy, higher inflation and REER appreciation," Tarapore added.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said the RBI has largely been absent since mid-March to stem the rupee's rise as it wants to tackle inflation. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market adds to liquidity fanning inflation.   \par \par \uc2 He said the RBI can consider incremental cash reserve ratio hikes to counter the criticism the ratio is a blunt instrument.   \par \par \uc2 The incremental cash reserve ratio immobilizes excess liquidity from where it is lodged rather than the average ratio which impounds equally from banks that are slower growing as well as banks that are faster growing.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI has raised the cash reserve ratio thrice by 50 basis points each since early December to the current level of 6.5%.   \par \par \uc2 The federal government has also raised the ceiling for market stabilization scheme bonds to INR1.1 trillion from INR850 billion earlier.   \par \par \uc2 Tarapore said real interest rates are still low in India and the scope for further monetary tightening still exists as signs of overheating remain visible.   \par \par \uc2 "Fundamentals do not warrant Indian interest rates going down. Over the recent period of monetary tightening, real interest rates have gone lower," Tarapore said.   \par \par \uc2 He added that sustaining a higher growth rate would require the current account deficit to rise.   \par \par \uc2 "Once you reach a 5%-6% current account deficit, all the rating agencies will say India is a dangerous country. It has got an unsustainable current account," he said.   \par \par \uc2 "If the stock market is booming, India runs a huge current account deficit and the rupee appreciates, the rational thing for a foreign institutional investor will be to take money out," he added.   \par \par \uc2 India is one of the few emerging economies to run a current account deficit, which stood at $11.8 billion for the April-December period of the last financial year.   \par \par \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  estimates current account deficit at 1.2% of the gross domestic product for the financial year ended March 31. Government data on the deficit for the last fiscal year are due later this month.   \par \par \uc2 -By Subhadip Sircar and Vipin Nair; Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 22884212; subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Alpana Sarma [ 21-05-07 0807GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070521e35l000f5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
