\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee eyes 9-yr peak as cash stays tight   \b0\par\par\uc2 285 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2007\par 05:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to open)   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, May 16 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee headed towards last week's nine-year high on Wednesday as investors sold dollars to generate funds to tide over a cash squeeze in the money market, and traders said capital inflows also helped the gains.   \par \par \uc2 At 10 a.m. (0430 GMT) the partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> was at 40.77/78 per dollar, moving up from Tuesday's close of 40.9550/9700. The rupee hit 40.53 early last week, its strongest since May 1998, according to Reuters data.   \par \par \uc2 "If the rupee breaks 40.80 conclusively, we could see it gain sharply on the day," said a dealer with a private bank.   \par \par \uc2 Overnight interbank rates <INROND=> were trading at 9.0-9.2 percent on Wednesday, reflecting tight money market conditions, and spurred demand for the local unit.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee has risen more than 8 percent against the dollar this year to be Asia's best-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Still, traders said demand for dollars from oil refiners could limit the rupee's gains.   \par \par \uc2 London Brent crude <LCOc1> held near $68 after a rally on further supply disruptions in Nigeria, the world's eighth-largest producer.   \par \par \uc2 Concerns over possible Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to thwart the rupee's rise appeared to wane in the market.   \par \par \uc2 Data released on Monday showed the RBI bought $2.3 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March, but traders believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has acted less aggressively in April and May.    \p