\par\par \b \uc2 Confident Indian rupee heads back towards 9-yr peak   \b0\par\par\uc2 297 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 2007\par 05:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to open)   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, May 14 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee headed back towards last week's 9-year peak on Monday, tracking gains in other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar, with dealers looking to test the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resolve to block its path.   \par \par \uc2 At 9:45 a.m. (0415 GMT), the partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> was at 40.957/967 per dollar, rising sharply from Friday's close of 41.22/23. The rupee touched 40.53 early last week, its strongest since May 1998, according to Reuters data.   \par \par \uc2 "The market was sitting a bit too long, fearing the RBI," said the chief dealer at a private bank, referring to prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "If it's not seen today, dealers will likely start betting on the rupee again," added the dealer, who expects the rupee to trade in a 40.90-41.15 range on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee has risen about 8 percent against the dollar this year, the biggest gain among Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The RBI bought $19.7 billion in the four months to end-February in a bid to thwart the rupee's gains, but traders say it has not been an aggressive buyer of dollars recently.   \par \par \uc2 Sentiment on the rupee was bolstered by data on Friday that showed India's industrial output in March rose an annual 12.9 percent, above a forecast for 10.4 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Many in the market have been expecting further monetary tightening in the next month or so, following on from measures taken since December.    \p