\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee hovers near 9-yr high; cbank watched   \b0\par\par\uc2 348 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2007\par 05:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, May 8 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee crept towards a nine-year high on Tuesday, although the market remained cautious about provoking \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fearing a sharp reversal of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent gains.   \par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> ended at 40.8000/8150 per dollar, ticking up from Monday's close of 40.87/89.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee hit 40.53 per dollar on Monday, its highest since May 1998, according to Reuters data, before skidding lower in late trade -- which some traders suspected was due to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "After what happened yesterday, the market was in the mood to consolidate. People don't know whether there's going to be a correction, or if the rupee's going to continue appreciating," said the chief dealer with a foreign bank.   \par \par \uc2 "We're searching for fresh cues, and in the absence of any, the market will remain rangebound," the dealer added.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee has gained more than 8 percent this year, with most of the gains coming in the last two months, boosted by capital inflows into the fast-growing economy. Foreign funds have bought more than $2.9 billion worth of stocks so far in 2007.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $19.7 billion in the four months to the end of February in a bid to stem the rupee's rise, but traders say it has stayed away from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market recently.   \par \par \uc2 The lack of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted Standard Chartered to revise its outlook for the rupee to 41.80 per dollar for the quarter ending September from a prior forecast of 43.60.   \par \par \uc2 "The Reserve Bank of India appears to be tolerating a much greater degree of rupee strength than we had previously expected," said Sundeep Bhandari, regional head of global markets, South Asia, at Standard Chartered, in a recent note.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par                    MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par --------------------------------------------------------------\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070511e35800075\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
