\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Which Indian Stocks To Consider Heading Into Monsoon Season   \b0\par\par\uc2 850 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Mai 2007\par 22:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Jay Somaney   \par \par \uc2 PLANO, Texas (Dow Jones) -- "Sell in May," advises the age-old adage, "and go away." This May, though, the adage does not fit, especially as far as Indian stocks are concerned.   \par \par \uc2 Let me outline the reasons, and then we will examine each one in detail:   \par \par \uc2 * Anticipation of the monsoons.   \par \par \uc2 * Most Indian stocks that trade on U.S. exchanges have barely budged, despite very good earnings due to a rising rupee.   \par \par \uc2 * The weakest quarter for Indian information-technology companies is the March quarter, and that is now behind us.   \par \par \uc2 * Despite a rising rupee, guidance from most Indian companies for the June quarter and for the fiscal year ending March 2008 was very good, all things considered.   \par \par \uc2 * The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and that is why the rupee has appreciated as much as it has.   \par \par \uc2 * Interest rates have more than likely peaked in India.   \par \par \uc2 Rain   \par \par \uc2 The monsoons are very important to the Indian economy, as a large portion of Indians are still considered rural residents who are very dependent on agriculture for their livelihood. A good rainy season means the power of the rural consumer is unleashed with purchases of farming equipment, motorcycles, automobiles, trucks, cell phones, clothes, jewelry and much else.   \par \par \uc2 The folks at the weather department have forecast a seasonal monsoon this year. That basically means that the amount of rainfall across India will vary no more than 10% up or down from normal, which is great news.   \par \par \uc2 For most Indian and quasi-Indian IT companies, the quarter ending in March is the seasonally weakest one, as annual wage hikes and increased visa costs lead to compressed margins.   \par \par \uc2 We are now entering the sweet spot for these companies, as the September and December quarters are usually the best.   \par \par \uc2 Buying when no one else is buying is how the easy money is made. By the time we are in the third and fourth quarters, these stocks' levels should be a lot higher than at the moment.   \par \par \uc2 Markets are currently fixated on the rising rupee, and as a result, despite stellar results and guidance from the Indian and quasi-Indian IT sector, the stocks have done very little. A major reason for the U.S. dollar's drastic decline vis-\u195\'00\'C3 -vis the Indian rupee has been a lack of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is in a fight against inflation. As a result, the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has refrained from buying dollars and weakening the rupee. As soon as inflation comes back in, the RBI will be forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and start buying dollars in order to keep Indian goods and services competitive in the global marketplace.   \par \par \uc2 There was also a lot of hand wringing in the media and among the dark siders about how these Indian and quasi-Indian IT companies are facing slowing demand and stagnant pricing for on-site and offshore work.   \par \par \uc2 Nothing could be further from the truth. Actually, demand is still very strong, and even if the U.S. economy drops into a recession the demand for Indian IT; business-process outsourcing, or BPO; and offshoring will only get stronger as American companies look for ways to decrease costs. Most of these companies are seeing across-the-board increases in prices paid ranging from up 2% to as much as 5%, depending on which company you look at and whether it's for on-site or offshore work.   \par \par \uc2 Finally, interest rates in India have more than likely peaked after a series of rate hikes strung together by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the beginning of this year. Between December 2006 and March 2007, Indian interest rates are up almost 250 basis points. Since February 2006, interest rates have been hiked by 400 basis points. More than likely, we have seen the last of these hikes in India, which means a major headwind will be removed from the markets.   \par \par \uc2 The Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates unchanged at its last meeting, April 24. It also revealed its annual policy statement for the year ending March 2008 and forecast GDP growth of around 8.5% and inflation to around 5% for this fiscal year.   \par \par \uc2 At present the bank rate stands at 6%, the reverse repo rate at 6% as well, the repo rate at 7.75% and the cash reserve ratio at 6.5%, and I don't think they will rise from these levels.   \par \par \uc2 Five to consider and four to avoid   \par \par \uc2 So, given the backdrop heading into the month, here are the five Indian and quasi-Indian companies to consider buying in May:   \par \par \uc2 * Cognizant Technology Solutions .   \par \par \uc2 * Infosys Technologies .   \par \par \uc2 * Satyam Computer Services .   \par \par \uc2 * Icici Bank .   \par \par \uc2 * Patni Computer Systems .   \par \par \uc2 These are the four Indian companies to avoid in May, waiting for a better entry point later in the year:   \par \par \uc2 * Tata Motors Ltd. .   \par \par \uc2 * Dr Reddys Labs .   \par \par \uc2 * Rediff Com India Ltd. .   \par \par \uc2 * Sify Ltd. .   \par \par \uc2 In summation, the sell-in-May-and-go-away mantra should be applied only selectively this year. And, until next time, happy investing. 