\par\par \b \uc2 V. PHANI KUMAR'S THIS WEEK IN INDIA: Have Investors Misread  \uc2 Tata Tea\uc2 's Intentions?   \b0\par\par\uc2 778 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 2007\par 02:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By V. Phani Kumar   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones) -- Investors in  \uc2 Tata Tea Ltd.\uc2  may have heaved a sigh of relief in the past week after its shares were boosted by an unconfirmed report in U.S. media that  \uc2 Coca Cola Co.\uc2  is eyeing an acquisition of enhanced-water brand Glaceau.   \par \par \uc2 But whether those gains will be sustained is a completely different matter.   \par \par \uc2 Tata Tea\uc2 's shares have been depressed ever since its fateful announcement in August of plans to buy a 30% stake for $677 million in Energy Brands, the unlisted firm that owns Glaceau. Over the next few months, the shares lost heavily as investors worried over the steep $2.2 billion valuation of Energy Brands and the impact of the acquisition on its financial profile.   \par \par \uc2 Against that backdrop, the market read big-and-mighty Coke's (KO) reported interest in buying Glaceau for around $3.5 billion as a compliment to the Tatas' decision to move in early and as a vindication of their investment strategy.   \par \par \uc2 Even so, the 14% surge in  \uc2 Tata Tea\uc2 's shares in the past week may have been overdone. Here's why:   \par \par \uc2 Some market participants seem to believe, perhaps naively, that the Tatas will cash out smilingly if Coke or someone else makes an attractive offer. I don't see how that's going to happen.   \par \par \uc2 Glaceau is a rapidly growing brand in the world's largest consumer market, with a product line-up targeted at the every-increasing population of health-conscious individuals. It also complements  \uc2 Tata Tea\uc2 's beverages and perfectly suits the Tatas' global ambitions. With just a 30% stake now, the Tatas may not have management control in Energy Brands, but certainly view it as a strategic investment.   \par \par \uc2 Given that, why anyone would think that the Tatas may behave like a venture fund or private-equity investor and sell out beats me.   \par \par \uc2 Even if one supposes that  \uc2 Tata Tea\uc2  may find any forthcoming offers too much to resist, where will it invest the proceeds? In a similarly strong and growing brand in the U.S. or Western Europe, I would think. Why then, would they exit Glaceau in the first place, nipping the bud before the blossom?   \par \par \uc2 The man in the hot seat is obviously Gateau's founder and CEO, J. Darius Bikoff, and it all boils down to whether he is attracted to any fresh offers that might come Glaceau's way. The Tatas, meanwhile, will no doubt ponder whether they should exercise the option they have to buy an additional 10% stake in Glaceau. And that brings us back to  \uc2 Tata Tea\uc2 's shares.   \par \par \uc2 If the Tatas do exercise that option or decide to raise their stake further through negotiations, it will mean further cash outflows. And that could bring another round of correction to the shares, especially if the Tatas don't get a majority control.   \par \par \uc2 There could be myriad other possibilities, including one where Tata agrees to stay on as a minority shareholder while Glaceau's control passes to someone other than the current management. It's difficult to predict the outcome of such an event today, but what is clear to me in the absence of further updates is that  \uc2 Tata Tea\uc2 's shares have jumped the gun and may be asking for a pullback.   \par \par \uc2 Rupee's strength vs. dollar spells doom for small exporters   \par \par \uc2 With the specter of a strengthening rupee against the U.S. dollar looming large over Indian exporters, investors in some of these companies will continue to be a worried lot in days to come. And it isn't just the software companies, which face little competition from regional peers and are more assured of continued order flows.   \par \par \uc2 Worse-hit are the hundreds of small-scale companies, especially textile exporters, who typically compete with exporters from other low-cost countries where the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation hasn't been as much in the past few months.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar, which could buy around 45.61 rupees in August, is currently worth just 41.10 rupees, due in part to a broad weakness for the greenback against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the world and in part to uninterrupted capital and fund inflows.   \par \par \uc2 What makes matters worse for these small exporters is that the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't showing any signs it'll \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and prevent further strengthening of the rupee. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is waging a war of its own against excessive liquidity inflation, and is worried that purchasing dollars to support the greenback will only boost the supply of rupees further. [ 04-29-07 2048ET ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020070430e34u00002\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
