\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-India cbank holds rates, acts against high inflows   \b0\par\par\uc2 By V. Ramakrishnan and John Mair   \par \uc2 727 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 April 2007\par 08:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with poll, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, prices)   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, April 24 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took steps to ease the upward pressure on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from capital inflows while leaving its key lending rate unchanged at 7.75 percent as expected at a policy review on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 However, the rupee jumped to a nine-year high of 41.04 per dollar <INR=IN>, stocks <.BSESN> ended 1.5 percent up and bond yields fell as the market welcomed the rate pause and reckoned the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not act aggressively to check growth.   \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of India (RBI), which has raised the lending rate five times in less than a year, forecast Asia's fourth-largest economy would grow 8.5 percent in the fiscal year ending March 2008, lower than 9.2 percent expected for 2006/07.   \par \par \uc2 Its last rate increase was at the end of March, and it said its focus was still on the fight against inflation rather than on economic growth at present.   \par \par \uc2 It aimed for wholesale price inflation, currently above 6 percent on an annual basis, of near 5.0 percent by next March but said rising prices still posed the main risk to the economy.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts said its main focus was to offset the impact of foreign capital inflows that have complicated its efforts to curb inflation and credit growth, as higher interest rates have simply attracted more capital.   \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows can potentially reduce the efficacy of monetary policy tightening by expanding liquidity," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in its annual policy review.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have recently poured billions of dollars into India's booming property and stock markets and into projects to expand production capacity to meet rapidly growing domestic demand.   \par \par \uc2 This has pushed up the rupee, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , concerned about a widening trade gap and the risk of a sudden reversal of flows, bought a massive $11.9 billion in February alone to block the rise. But its actions have put more rupees into the domestic money supply, adding to inflation pressures.   \par \par \uc2 Rob Subbaraman at Lehman Brothers in Hong Kong said many of the new measures addressed the strong capital inflows.   \par \par \uc2 "(They) seem to be a growing issue or concern for policy makers in India, and will continue to be a big issue," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Readying for further rupee strength, the RBI said it was also raising the limit on bonds it uses to neutralise \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 EASING THE RULES   \par \par \uc2 Thailand too has been troubled by strong foreign inflows and slapped on controls last year.   \par \par \uc2 But, mindful that India needs foreign money to build up its infrastructure, the RBI opted instead to allow more domestic outflows, noting infrastructure bottlenecks were a constraint on the economy.   \par \par \uc2 It said moves to greater capital account convertibility might accelerate and proposed letting firms repay more foreign debt sooner, as well as raising the aggregate ceiling for overseas investment by mutual funds.   \par \par \uc2 It raised a limit for firms' portfolio investment abroad and proposed a higher cap on their general overseas investment as well as cutting the rate ceiling on non-resident deposits.   \par \par \uc2 It aimed to contain money supply growth at 17-17.5 percent in 2007/08, below the actual rate of about 20 percent.   \par \par \uc2 TIME FOR A BREAK   \par \par \uc2 Analysts polled by Reuters after the decision expected at least one more tightening, although a pause was possible as inflation and credit growth showed signs of slowing.   \par \par \uc2 "The RBI has kept the door open for any swift measure, and it could be warranted by anything, but we believe it won't be needed in the near term," said Harish Menon at  \uc2 ING Vysya Bank.\par \par \uc2 The RBI kept its key borrowing rate, known as the reverse repo, steady at 6.0 percent and held the bank rate, used to price long-term lending, steady at 6.0 percent.   \par \par \uc2 There was no change to banks' cash reserve ratio beyond a previously announced increase to 6.5 percent due on April 28. It has raised the ratio three times since mid-December in an attempt to curb credit growth of about 30 percent a year.   \par \par \uc2 INDIA-CBANK/ (UPDATE 3, PICTURE)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|SNI|ABX|RNP|DNP|PCM|PEN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070424e34o000l0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
