\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee takes a pause after sharp rally   \b0\par\par\uc2 342 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 2007\par 07:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, April 19 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee ended little changed on Thursday as demand for dollars from importers was offset by capital inflows into Asia's fourth-largest economy, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 Concerns over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  made traders circumspect about building large positions in the rupee, which kept it from testing Tuesday's nine-year high of 41.62 per dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee <INR=IN> ended at 42.065/080 per dollar, a shade weaker than Wednesday's 42.05/07.   \par \par \uc2 "This is a natural pause after a some very volatile sessions," said a dealer at a private bank, who expects the rupee to trade in a broad 41.80-42.20 range over the next week.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee is up 3 percent this month and is trading nearly 12 percent above a three-year low of 47.04 hit last July, boosted by strong capital inflows into Asia's fourth-largest economy.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign funds have poured in $2.4 billion into Indian equities this year, helping fuel the rupee's gains.   \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) is thought to have eased back on its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb rupee gains in the past few weeks, but traders say it still may be uncomfortable with the pace of gains as a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dents exporters' competitiveness.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI bought a record $11.9 billion in February in a bid to stem the rupee's rise. It bought $19.7 billion in the four months to the end of February.   \par \par \uc2 "I see the starting to RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, as the rupee's strength is getting reflected in the export growth deceleration, and we can't afford that as an emerging economy," said Rupa Rege Nitsure, chief economist at  \uc2 Bank of Baroda\uc2 , who expects the rupee will stabilise between 43-43.5 by July.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par                    MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|RBN|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070422e34j0002i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
