\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Fears As  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  Rupee Nears 9-Yr Highs   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Subhadip Sircar   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 950 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2007\par 12:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--After a rush of capital inflows spiked the Indian rupee to multiyear highs, concerns are rising the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may take a more aggressive approach in reining in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The rupee's surge to near nine-year highs from three-year lows last July is hurting exporter earnings, and with inflation starting to ease, the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have room to resume \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after staying away since February. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could also take steps to counter capital inflows, analysts said.   \par \par \uc2 Thus, in the near term, the dollar is unlikely to breach the key INR41.00 support, and could instead bounce back up to INR42.50, they said. Over the longer term, the dollar could trend higher toward INR44.00, possibly by year end. Standard Chartered Bank, for instance, expects the dollar to end 2007 around INR43.80.   \par \par \uc2 "The RBI needs to aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a continuous rupee rise will have a deflationary impact on the economy and slow growth," said A.V.Rajwade, a member of the RBI's committee on fuller capital account convertibility.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee has risen nearly 9% since it hit a three-year low last July. At 1040 GMT Wednesday, the dollar was trading at INR42.02, near nine-year highs.   \par \par \uc2 Rupee strength has hit exporter earnings hard, particularly for the country's important software and textile firms, whose earnings are in dollars.   \par \par \uc2 "Export growth, in terms of the three-month moving average, has dipped to about 7% now from 32.3% in August. Part of this (lower growth) will be a lagged impact of a significant increase in the rupee," said Sujan Hajra, chief economist at Mumbai-based brokerage firm Anand Rathi Securities.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts reckon that a one-percentage-point gain in the rupee against the dollar shrinks Infosys Technologies' operating margin by 50 basis points and HCL Technologies' margins by 35 basis points. These are among two of India's largest software export firms.   \par \par \uc2 Likewise for the textile industry. "Operating margins for most apparel manufacturers are likely to fall to 2.5%-3% from around 6% currently due to the rupee rise and rising interest rates," said Vijay Agarwal, chairman at Apparel Exports Promotion Council, an industry body.   \par \par \uc2 Rupee strength not only hurts earnings but also Indian exporter competitiveness against major rivals such as Indonesia, China, and Bangladesh.   \par \par \uc2 Latest government data showed India's merchandise exports rose 7.9% on year to $9.70 billion in February while imports grew 25.1% to $14.36 billion in the month. For the April-February period, exports totaled $109.13 billion, suggesting that for the fiscal year ended March 31, exports likely failed to meet the government's target $125 billion.   \par \par \uc2 With export growth moderating, India's trade deficit for the April-December period was $52.3 billion, higher than the $40.1 billion gap for the previous full fiscal year. The current account, meanwhile, was in deficit of $11.8 billion for the April-December period.   \par \par \uc2 JP Morgan, which said its Real Effective Exchange Rate Index shows the rupee is overvalued by more than 13%, a record high, expects the current account deficit to widen to $14.3 billion for the last fiscal year and $19.9 billion this year.   \par \par \uc2 "The RBI should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately to stem the rupee rise. It is important to remember exports create employment, not imports," said Ajit Ranade, chief economist at AV Birla Group.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI tried to stem the rupee rise between November and February, buying $19.7 billion of dollars during that period. But when it stopped, the rupee shot higher.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI may have been keen to allow the rupee to gain as a way of controlling inflation. Rising prices became a hot political potato recently, with the ruling Congress party attributing its defeat in some recent state elections to higher prices.   \par \par \uc2 But inflation has begun to ease as a favorable statistical base effect comes into play, and recent fiscal and monetary measures start to pull prices lower.   \par \par \uc2 Inflation was 5.74% on year for the week ended March 31, easing from a two-year high of 6.73% for the week ended Feb. 3, and is expected to slip under 5.00% in the first half of this fiscal year.   \par \par \uc2 Apart from inflation, the RBI also stopped its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since February possibly because it has reached close to the INR800 billion limit on issuance of its market stabilization securities. Without the ability to issue more bonds, the RBI risks leaving its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilized, which would contribute to faster money supply growth and potentially higher inflation.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI and federal government had said a review the ceiling would be made when it breaches the INR700 billion mark. Analysts expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to review the ceiling when the RBI makes its annual policy review on April 24.   \par \par \uc2 Speculation has also heightened the RBI could announce measures to counter the heavy capital inflows such as restrictions on commercial borrowings by companies, and cutting interest rates on deposits offered to nonresident Indians.   \par \par \uc2 "The thrust of the policy will be on addressing structural issues that are causing a surge in capital inflows," said Shubhada Rao, chief economist at Yes Bank.   \par \par \uc2 Capital Inflows, primarily into India's equity markets but also from foreign direct investment, have helped the rupee rise sharply.   \par \par \uc2 India recently became the fifth Asian economy to cross the $200 billion level in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.   \par \par \uc2 -By Subhadip Sircar; Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 22884212; djn.in@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Lim Mui Khi [ 18-04-07 1041GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 4258 5095   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070418e34i000l7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
