\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Rupee Rises To Near 9Y High; Bonds Little Changed   \b0\par\par\uc2 519 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 2007\par 15:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                    As at 1200 GMT \par                    Latest     Change \par  USD/INR           41.90      -0.62 \par  8.07% 2017 Bond   8.09%      Unchanged \par  Call Rate*        7.50%      +200 BPs \par  \par  Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par  March             1.96       +0.8 \par  *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount is midpoint of bid-offer spreads. \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee rose close to a nine-year high against the dollar Monday amid demand from foreign investors keen to buy local stocks, but the rupee's gains are likely to be capped, traders said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at INR41.90, a level last seen in June 1998, as against INR42.52 at Friday's close.   \par \par \uc2 "Foreign fund inflows are strong and there is enough scope for the rupee to gain further. But I think we could see the dollar bouncing back in the coming session," said Parthasarathi Mukherjee at  \uc2 UTI Bank.\par \par \uc2 Mukherjee said the current level of the dollar would be attractive for importers, which could help it rise past INR42.   \par \par \uc2 "I expect the dollar to trade in a INR41.80-INR42.20 band in the near term," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the rupee surged largely due to robust foreign fund inflows into the stock market, which helped local shares to rise sharply.   \par \par \uc2 The Bombay Stock Exchange's benchmark Sensitive Index gained 311.50 points, or 2.3%, and closed at 13,695.58 Monday.   \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  largely refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the dollar, which accentuated the rupee's sharp rise.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the RBI's strategy seems to be to let the rupee gain as a stronger local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will help ease inflation by bringing down prices of imported goods.   \par \par \uc2 In the government bond market, the benchmark 8.07% 2017 bond's price ended nearly flat Monday at INR99.79 as against INR99.80 at Friday's close.   \par \par \uc2 Bond prices were marginally higher in the early session as the size of a government bond auction for the current week came in lower than expected by market players.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI said after market hours Friday that it would sell INR30 billion of 7.55% 2010 bonds Wednesday under its market stabilization scheme, or MSS.   \par \par \uc2 However, prices slipped later as the overnight call money rate began to rise, indicating that banking system surplus cash is dwindling after the 25- basis-point hike in banks' cash reserve ratio came into effect Saturday.   \par \par \uc2 The cash reserve ratio, currently at 6.25%, is the share of deposits banks must maintain with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 According to a foreign bank dealer, the 10-year bond's yield is likely to trade in a 8.10%-8.15% range in coming sessions as liquidity will tighten further after the MSS bond auctions.   \par \par \uc2 The overnight call money rate - the interbank lending rate - ended at 7.50% Monday after hitting a session high of 13%. The call rate had closed at 5.50% Friday.   \par \par \uc2 -By Vipin V. Nair, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 2288 4212; vipin.nair@dowjones.com [ 16-04-07 1300GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070416e34g000ox\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
