\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:India Is Fifth Asian Econ With Over $200B  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Reserves   \b0\par\par\uc2 422 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2007\par 14:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details about sources of reserve accretion, analyst comments.) .   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Friday that the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have crossed $200 billion, making it only the fifth Asian economy with a level that high.   \par \par \uc2 Reserves have risen sharply in recent months as the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem a more-than-9% rise in the value of the rupee since July, analysts say.   \par \par \uc2 The increase in reserves is bound to intensify debate on whether a part of the reserves should be invested in high-yielding assets, the returns on which can then be used fund infrastructure development in the country.   \par \par \uc2 The reserves are now invested mostly in low yielding U.S. government debt, but there have been recent calls for the reserves to be moved into equities overseas and real estate.   \par \par \uc2 "The productive use of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves over and above what the RBI thinks is (the minimum comfort level) necessary in its risk assessment is always an issue," said Samiran Chakraborty, chief economist at ICICI Bank.   \par \par \uc2 The latest federal budget suggests studying a proposal to set up two overseas entities to borrow \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the RBI. These entities could then fund long-term infrastructure projects.   \par \par \uc2 India's federal government estimates it will need investments of $320 billion during the next five years to improve the quality of infrastructure to match global standards.   \par \par \uc2 Some economists also feel that India could do with a slower pace of rise in reserves as it adds to inflationary pressures, requiring sterilization measures such as domestic bond issuances.   \par \par \uc2 "I think it is a number we are increasingly getting worried about. We could do with a slower growth in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," said Abheek Barua, chief economist at  \uc2 ABN Amro Bank.\par \par \uc2 ABN Amro estimates the cost of sterilization at 2% of gross domestic product.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI said earlier Friday that it bought $19.7 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  between November and February.   \par \par \uc2 Its weekly statistical supplement showed reserves rose by $1.141 billion for the week ended April 6 to $200.320 billion from a week earlier. Reserves have risen $46.111 billion in the past 12 months alone.   \par \par \uc2 China, Japan, South Korea and Taiwan are the other Asian economies to have reserves in excess of $200 billion.   \par \par \uc2 -By Subhadip Sircar and Vipin V. Nair; Dow Jones Newswires; 91 981-060-2808; djin.in@dowjones.com [ 13-04-07 1233GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070413e34d000kv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
