\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee falls 1.7pct, biggest drop in 9 years   \b0\par\par\uc2 419 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2007\par 05:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, March 29 (Reuters) - The rupee fell 1.7 percent on Thursday, posting its biggest single-day percentage loss in nearly nine years, on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and short-covering of dollar positions after the Indian unit had risen to a 7-year high on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said month end dollar buying by oil companies also pushed the rupee to its lowest close in more than a week.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee <INR=IN> ended at 43.770/785 per dollar, weaker than Wednesday's close of 43.04/06. It was the biggest percentage drop since May 14, 1998, when it had dropped 1.9 percent, Reuters data showed.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee has gained about 7.5 percent since hitting a three-year low last July. It touched a peak of 43.01 during trade on Wednesday, its highest level since November 1999, according to Reuters data.   \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively sold rupees between 43.30-50, which suggests the rally is over," said a dealer with a private bank.   \par \par \uc2 "Everyone was expecting a correction to happen, though its speed was a little surprising," added the dealer.   \par \par \uc2 Traders also said a shift in the settlement date for three-day forward contracts struck on Wednesday due to the end of the fiscal year was another reason behind the fall. India's fiscal year ends on March 31.   \par \par \uc2 "The premium was built into today's rate as there is no settlement of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deals on April 2 because of year-end account closing for banks," a foreign bank trader said.   \par \par \uc2 Call money rates traded at 10-11 percent on Thursday. They touched a decade high of 70 percent last week, as banks faced an acute cash crunch following advance tax outflows of about 300 billion rupees ($6.8 billion).   \par \par \uc2 Standard Chartered said that while the rupee's near-term strength may persist, it would fall to 45.50 by the end of June.   \par \par \uc2 "The excessive strength in the rupee has been caused by a liquidity squeeze... as government expenditure seeps back into the system, liquidity is unlikely to tighten much further," it said in a note on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 "Finally despite the fact that the RBI (Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) has so far tolerated rupee appreciation, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still possible," it said.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par                    MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE/ (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|ABN|M|SNI|RBN|ABX|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070401e33t0001x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
