\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee slips from 19-mnth high as cash eases  \b0\par\par\uc2 338 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2007\par 13:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, March 22 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee slipped from 19-month highs against the dollar on Thursday as tight money market conditions eased, and month-end demand for dollars from oil refiners kicked in, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Investors also squared off short positions in the dollar on fears the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been oversold, and that a sharp correction would ensue, possibly triggered by Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The rupee <INR=IN> ended at 43.73/74 per dollar, weakening 0.7 percent from Wednesday's 43.4450/4550, which was its strongest close since Aug. 1, 2005, according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 "People weren't convinced about the rupee's rally -- it was the pain on call rates that made people sell (dollars)," said a dealer with a private bank.  \par \par \uc2 "But over the last few years, everytime the market has approached the low 43s it has quickly moved in the other direction... a lot of people have absorbed that trend, and bargained to buy (dollars) today," the dealer added.  \par \par \uc2 The interbank rate <INROND=> ended at 10-15 percent, dropping from the previous close of 60-70 percent after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said banks could use funds borrowed in its repurchase auction for interbank lending.  \par \par \uc2 Traders also said that month-end demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from oil refiners was picking up, putting further pressure on the rupee.  \par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs said the rupee's spike was temporary, and expects it to trade at 44.15 within a 3-month target period.  \par \par \uc2 "We expect the rupee to depreciate as it is over-bought. Banks will likely start re-constituting their dollar assets, and the recent excessive demand for the rupee will moderate on the back of some easing of liquidity," the investment bank said in a note.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par                     MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE/ (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|SNI|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070325e33m000dj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
