\par\par \b \uc2 Cbank watched as Indian rupee nears 18-mth highs  \b0\par\par\uc2 344 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2007\par 04:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to open)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, March 20 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee hovered near an 18-month high against the dollar on Tuesday as buoyant Asian markets lifted sentiment, but traders were wary the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would block the rupee's path as it approached the 44 mark.  \par \par \uc2 At 9:35 a.m. (0405) GMT, the partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> was at 44.067/072 per dollar, rising from Friday's 44.11/12. The rupee market was shut on Monday for a holiday.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  "Asia is up ... most of the the indices are in the plus,\par and the stock market should follow -- there's a good chance the\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 rupee will test 44 today," said a dealer at a private bank, who expects the rupee to trade in a 44.03-44.13 range on Tuesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It all depends on what levels the RBI is comfortable with," the dealer said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rupee last breached the 44 mark in late Sep. 2005. It tested 44.03 per dollar last month before suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  thwarted its rise.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is suspected of selling the rupee to cap its gains and protect export competitiveness. Data shows the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has purchased close to $8 billion in the three months to January, and traders widely believe it has played an active role in capping the rupee in February too.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Tuesday, the rupee gained support as most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  held firm against the dollar as rallying global equity markets reflected greater investor appetite for risk.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign funds have poured in about 1.1 billion into Indian equities in 2007, which has helped power the rupee's gains.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rupee has strengthened by about 6.5 percent since hitting a 3-year trough last July, and it is thought the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is uneasy with the pace of the Indian unit's appreciation.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ----------------------------------------------------------------\par \par                      MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  ---------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|SNI|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070323e33k0005g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
