\par\par \b \uc2 India Bonds Fall On Higher Inflation; Rupee At 3-Week High  \b0\par\par\uc2 446 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2007\par 14:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                    As Of 1200 GMT \par                    Latest      Change \par  USD/INR           44.11       -0.09 \par  8.07% 2017 Bond   8.01%       +3BPs \par  Call Rate         10.00%      +450BPs \par  \par  Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par  February          INR1.41     +0.06 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount is midpoint of bid-offer spreads.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--Indian government bonds fell for the second straight session Friday as higher-than-expected inflation data sparked fears of more monetary tightening by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , market participants said.  \par \par \uc2 A squeeze in banking system cash due to quarterly advance tax payments by Indian companies is also likely to push bond prices down in the coming sessions, they said.  \par \par \uc2 The benchmark 8.07% 2017 bond ended at INR100.35, down from INR100.54 on Thursday's close in thin trade.  \par \par \uc2 "Trading volumes have dried up and there is no activity in the market now because of the lack of clarity on direction," said a foreign bank dealer.  \par \par \uc2 Wholesale price index-based inflation for the week ended March 3 rose to 6.46% on the year from 6.10% in the previous week. A Dow Jones Newswires poll had forecast inflation at 6.27%.  \par \par \uc2 The higher-than-expected inflation data prompted Indian Finance Minister P. Chidambaram to say that the government would continue to take steps to check rising prices.  \par \par \uc2 "Let me assure the honorable members that government has taken, and will take, fiscal, monetary and supply side measures to moderate inflation," Chidambaram told the lower house of Parliament.  \par \par \uc2 But despite the efforts by the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , headline inflation is likely to remain above 6.0% in the remaining part of the current fiscal year, said D. K. Joshi, principal economist at rating agency Crisil.  \par \par \uc2 "There will be some moderation (in the inflation rate), but it will be after April," said Joshi.  \par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the Indian rupee rose to a three-week high against the dollar as banks sold the greenback to generate cash in the face of the tightening liquidity.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended Friday's session at INR44.11, as against INR44.20 late Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "The call money rate has shot up and that makes holding long dollar positions costlier," said one foreign bank dealer.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in the coming sessions to stem further appreciation of the rupee.  \par \par \uc2 The call money rate nearly doubled to 10% Friday from Thursday, reflecting strong demand for funds in the market.  \par \par \uc2 -By Vipin V. Nair; Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 2288 4212; vipin.nair@dowjones.com [ 16-03-07 1335GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070316e33g000ot\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
