\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee ends higher, away from 2007 low  \b0\par\par\uc2 291 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2007\par 04:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, March 6 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee ended higher on Tuesday, recovering from the previous session's 2007 low after a firm trend in Asian financial markets encouraged dollar sales by local exporters.  \par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> ended at 44.46/47 per dollar, stronger than 44.625/640 at close on Monday when it had hit an intraday trough of 44.695, its lowest since Dec. 21. The rupee rose after most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebounded as stock markets in the region posted gains after heavy recent losses.  \par \par \uc2 "There was heavy dollar selling on expectations that a rise in Asian stocks may encourage capital inflows in the coming weeks," a state-run bank trader said.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign funds have bought a net $1.26 billion in Indian equities in 2007, after nearly $8 billion of investments in 2006.  \par \par \uc2 The benchmark BSE stock index <.BSESN> ended more than 2 percent higher on Tuesday. It had fallen 3.7 percent on Monday to its lowest close in five months, extending last week's 5.5 percent slide. Traders, however remained wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to maintain \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export competitiveness. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys dollars through a clutch of state-run banks.  \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month to pull the rupee down from a 16-month high of 44.03.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  -------------------